    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4a226afe-e9e4-5edc-858e-f95502a9f1a1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(S)=-30$\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=-20$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)(4+6)+2(8-3)+2(8+2) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =-20+10+20 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=10\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "763ce6f9-b4ce-5c82-a2b3-071f2d0103a0",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
