    "question": {
      ":question/id": "13a1e1a3-dfb7-50fa-a14b-729384757320",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(N)=2$\n- **II.** $det(N)=-30$\n- **III.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(9+1)+0+0 \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =-30 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "56824113-7199-5e8a-80a8-1147182167ac",
