  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d1efdfc-c742-5c82-9bdc-2ea0a10f68fb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(E)=5$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3-2)+0+(-1)(-2+3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cc4d59c0-5d98-506d-90c6-5aaac7f459fa",
