  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5280874e-5868-50a3-88ee-947ee266372a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=24$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(15+9)+(-3)(5-9)+1(3+9) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 24+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-24+12+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c00448cb-53bf-5262-ba0b-c0081073e59e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
