  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "725fd4ee-e2b2-5548-943c-ae413817942d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(D)=0$\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=48$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(8-6)+(-2)(8-12)+(-2)(-8+16) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =8+8-16 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ab68621a-3a4c-57b4-812a-4a3eb617014c",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
