  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "060bba92-691d-5bfb-8f5e-117d13075980",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=3$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0-6)+0+3(6+0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-18+18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bcd393b9-424e-5751-99b2-5e1753987e47",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
