    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "eaa8fc44-460c-5bc5-b26d-657280bd7157",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(D)=-3$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-6)+2(-4+8)+1(-6+12) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =8+6 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=14\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "62c9c874-4ced-505c-bdfa-9832ed100363",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
