    "question": {
      ":question/id": "63d38875-75ff-58a3-a18f-2746c8292472",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(P)=-2$\n- **II.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(P)=0$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+1(9-9)+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "34d0e4f3-99f6-5f52-9ca0-3a2e6c037e43",
