  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "25ccf6f1-2a2a-5423-93ce-2bafede911b6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=20$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-9+0)+(-4)(-6+0)+(-3)(14-9) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-9)+(-4)\\cdot (-6)+(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-9+24-15 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cfa53fa2-2c58-5fc8-ba1c-66292ae2eb68",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
