    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ba767f55-b1a5-5378-9242-1d52522a9442",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(D)=-8$\n- **III.** $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(4+0)+0+(-1)(0+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9c517e21-259f-5d41-bee4-bd9d681da9f7",
